# Slice 333 (the author 2026-11-09: `fn: new_name merge(f1, f2)`): the name is given apart -- written, not evaluated --;
# merge composes an ordinary Structure from the parts of two created functions (f2 is a descriptor without a body, so
# the parts args and data are composed and the code is f1's), and fn creates from it the function g in the namespace of
# this traversal. f1 and f2 have no field name: the name is the receiver's argument. g takes two arguments where f1
# takes one; g's data is the merged data completed by the own fields of the body (seen), by the one constructor of every
# form; f1 and f2 stay as written, and their counters stay theirs.
int: k 2
fn: f1 (int: a) int (int: calls 0)
    calls := calls + 1
    int: seen a
    return: a * 10 + k
end: f1
fn: f2 (int: a; int: b) int
fn: g merge(f1, f2)
k := 5
int: r1 f1(1)
int: r2 g(1; 2)
int: r3 g(3; 4)
int: c1 f1\data\calls
int: c2 g\data\calls
int: s1 f1\data\seen
int: s2 g\data\seen
